  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1125 PM CDT WED SEP 30 2026\n\nMoisture will continue to stream into the region via a stiff\nsouth wind from the Gulf, with dry conditions persisting the next\nfew days. This gradual moisture return into the region will be\nthe precursor to our upcoming rain event this weekend, as PWAT\nvalues still need to climb in order to meet our current QPF\nforecast. The other caveat here will be the placement of the\nstalled front through the weekend, with slight deviations\nresulting in substantial changes in overall rainfall amounts.\n\nAt this time, confidence is still medium that the approaching\ncold front will stall in a very favorable location north of the\nstate, resulting in widespread 1-2 inches of rainfall through\nMonday. The bulk of this rainfall will fall between Saturday and\nSunday, when a weak shortwave moves inland from the Gulf Coast.\nGiven the moderate to heavy nature of this anticipated rain,\nlocally higher amounts above two inches are very likely, with\ncurrent probabilities of exceedance near 50% for most of the\nstate. As previously mentioned though, a good chunk of these\nhigher rainfall amounts depend on location of the stalled cold\nfront. By Monday, a deepening trough over eastern Canada will\nkick this cold front back into gear, pushing it through the\nregion by early Tuesday morning. This will allow cool, dry air\nback into the area, ushering in another period of much more\nseasonable temperatures through the middle of next week.\n\n/44/",
